<ResourceDictionary xmlns="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml/presentation"
                    xmlns:x="http://schemas.microsoft.com/winfx/2006/xaml">

  <!--
    The feel, as shared values.

    Every curve and duration in the card resolves here. The point is not that these numbers are
    special on their own - it is that one-off values are what make an interface read as assembled
    rather than designed, and a curve two hundredths out feels subtly wrong without being nameable.

    Colour, type and geometry live in CardTokens.xaml; this dictionary is additive and owns only
    easing, duration and the press response.
  -->

  <!-- The house curve. Default for almost everything. -->
  <KeySpline x:Key="EaseSmooth" ControlPoint1="0.22,1" ControlPoint2="0.36,1" />



  <!--
    There is deliberately no spring curve here. A KeySpline clamps its control points to 0..1 on
    both axes, unlike a CSS cubic-bezier, so an easing curve cannot overshoot at all. A pop comes
    from overshooting the animated VALUE - scale 1 to 1.05 to 1, rising on EaseOut and settling on
    EaseSmooth. Never encode a bounce in the
    curve; it silently clamps and you get a flat move that looks like a mistake.
  -->



  <!--
    A firm press, not a collapse. The card shipped with 0.94, which on a 44 epx circle is a 2.6 epx
    shrink - large enough to read as the control flinching away from the finger rather than taking
    it. 0.98 is felt and barely seen, which is the whole idea.
  -->
  <x:Double x:Key="PressScale">0.98</x:Double>


</ResourceDictionary>
